    "report": "# Research-Gap Report: Medical Image Segmentation\n\n---\n\n## Topic Landscape\n\nThe medical image segmentation literature clusters strongly around transformer and attention mechanisms (51 papers, mean year 2022.1), which now dominate over the earlier U-Net/architecture tradition (31 papers, mean year 2020.6). Surrounding these pillars are active mid-sized clusters on semi-supervised and contrastive learning (14 papers each, ~2021\u20132022), the Segment Anything Model (SAM, 14 papers, mean year 2023.3), few-shot learning (9 papers), and the emerging Mamba/state-space direction (9 papers, mean year 2024.2), indicating a field rapidly diversifying from pure architectural innovation toward data-efficiency, foundation-model adaptation, and novel sequence-modeling paradigms.\n\n---\n\n## Validated Gaps\n\n### Gap 1 \u2014 Transformer Attention Mechanisms for Zero-Shot / SAM-Based Segmentation\n**Why it is a gap:** The transformer cluster (size 51) and the SAM/zero-shot cluster (size 14) are linked by 282 bridge papers yet share a prominence score of only 0.12, and targeted verification queries (\"transformer attention\" + \"segment anything model\"; \"global dependencies\" + \"zero-shot segmentation\") returned just **2 recent papers** \u2014 the lowest retrieval count among all candidates \u2014 yielding a high confidence verdict of **CONFIRMED (0.92)**. The near-absence of recent work directly coupling transformer attention design with SAM-style zero-shot inference reveals a clear underexplored intersection.\n\n**Research Agenda:**\n1. *Characterise the gap empirically:* Systematically benchmark existing transformer-based segmentation architectures (e.g., Swin-UNet, TransUNet) on zero-shot transfer tasks using SAM evaluation protocols to quantify the performance ceiling without task-specific fine-tuning.\n2. *Develop bridging mechanisms:* Design lightweight adapter or prompt-injection layers that translate learned transformer attention maps into SAM-compatible sparse/dense prompts, enabling zero-shot generalisation across imaging modalities and anatomical targets.\n3. *Validate clinically:* Evaluate the proposed hybrid on held-out, low-resource clinical datasets (rare pathologies, novel scanners) where annotated data is scarce, measuring Dice, HD95, and annotation-cost reduction relative to fully supervised baselines.\n\n---\n\n### Gap 2 \u2014 Transformer Global Dependencies Combined with Edge-Attention / Classification Modules\n**Why it is a gap:** The transformer/attention cluster (size 51) and the edge-attention/network/proposed cluster (size 11) share 282 bridge papers and a prominence of 0.10, yet verification queries (\"transformer attention\" + \"edge attention classification\"; \"global dependencies\" + \"attention module network\") returned **0 recent papers** \u2014 an absolute absence of recent targeted work \u2014 giving this gap the highest confidence of any candidate: **CONFIRMED (1.0)**. Transformers excel at capturing long-range global dependencies but are well-documented to underperform on fine-grained boundary and edge detail; the edge-attention literature addresses precisely that weakness, yet the two have not been integrated in recent work.\n\n**Research Agenda:**\n1. *Failure-mode analysis:* Quantify boundary-region segmentation errors (e.g., boundary F1, surface Dice) for state-of-the-art transformer segmentation models versus CNN-based edge-attention networks on standard benchmarks (e.g., ACDC, Synapse multi-organ) to formally document the complementarity.\n2. *Unified architecture design:* Develop a dual-branch or hierarchical network in which transformer encoder blocks supply global context while a parallel edge-attention module (trained with explicit boundary supervision) refines local structure, with a learned fusion gate controlling their interaction.\n3. *Generalisation study:* Test the integrated model across multiple imaging modalities (MRI, CT, ultrasound) and report both region-level (Dice) and boundary-level metrics, with ablations isolating the contribution of each component to confirm the gap's practical significance.\n\n---\n\n## Rejected Candidates\n\nFive candidate gaps \u2014 transformer \u00d7 U-Net/architecture (1,296 recent papers), transformer \u00d7 deep-learning reviews (294), transformer \u00d7 annotation/training (432), transformer \u00d7 3D/volumetric (216), and transformer \u00d7 domain adaptation (776) \u2014 were rejected due to substantial existing recent literature already covering these intersections."
